</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We present TASC, a <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">T</span>ask-<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>ware <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">S</span>hared <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">C</span>ontrol framework for teleoperated manipulation that infers task-level user intent and provides assistance throughout the task. To support everyday tasks without predefined knowledge, TASC constructs an open-vocabulary interaction graph from visual input to represent functional object relationships, and infers user intent accordingly. A shared control policy then provides rotation assistance during both grasping and object interaction, guided by spatial constraints predicted by a vision-language model. Our method addresses two key challenges in general-purpose, long-horizon shared control: (1) understanding and inferring task-level user intent, and (2) generalizing assistance across diverse objects and tasks. Experiments in both simulation and the real world demonstrate that TASC improves task efficiency and reduces user input effort compared to prior methods. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first shared control framework that supports everyday manipulation tasks with zero-shot generalization. The code that supports our experiments is publicly available at <a href="https://github.com/fitz0401/tasc" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">https://github.com/fitz0401/tasc</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">INTRODUCTION</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Teleoperation enables human operators to control robots in unstructured or safety-critical environments, where full autonomy remains unreliable due to perceptual uncertainty (e.g., underwater exploration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>), task ambiguity (e.g., assistive robotics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>), or the need for human judgment (e.g., surgical robotics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>). Although easy to deploy, direct operation of high-degree-of-freedom (DoF) robotic arms remains cognitively demanding and physically fatiguing, especially for novice operators. To reduce this burden, shared control frameworks blend user input with autonomous assistance, leveraging sensor feedback to filter user input noise and improve precision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A shared control system assists by interpreting the user’s intent, which can be expressed through various modalities: language is well suited for specifying high-level task goals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, eye gaze can indicate object-level interest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, and hand-operated devices (e.g., joysticks and keyboards) are effective for conveying motion-level preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. In this work, we focus on intent expressed exclusively through hand-operated input. This choice is motivated by the fact that motion control is a natural way for humans to execute tasks, offering strong procedural compatibility and precise control over robot behavior. We believe that shared control should be fundamentally driven by motion-based input, with high-level modalities such as language serving as optional complements once intent inference from low-level signals is well established.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing shared control methods based on motion input remain far from providing general-purpose, long-horizon assistance in everyday environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. We identify two key challenges that hinder progress:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Task understanding and inference.</span> Inferring user intent from motion commands is difficult in multi-step everyday tasks. For example, pouring water involves grasping a kettle, moving it over a cup, and executing the pour. To support such tasks, shared control systems must identify the current task and recognize task progression.
<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Task generalization.</span> A general-purpose shared control system should assist in diverse tasks involving varied objects. Prior works are largely restricted to well-structured problems, such as target reaching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and grasping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, which struggle to generalize to everyday manipulation involving diverse object-object interactions and high-DoF continuous action spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in vision-language models (VLMs) offer a promising direction for general-purpose, long-horizon shared control. Trained on large-scale multimodal data, VLMs encode rich commonsense knowledge about object semantics and interactions. They can infer functional relationships between objects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and reason about plausible tasks from a scene <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this insight, we propose TASC (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), a task-aware shared control framework that supports task-level intent inference and assistance throughout task execution. Our system consists of two key components:

<br class="ltx_break">(1) First, we construct an <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">open-vocabulary interaction graph</span> capturing possible object-object relationships and corresponding interaction constraints through VLMs. This graph representation helps our system to understand the composition and progression of multi-step tasks. Unlike prior approaches that focus only on short-term goals, our method enables inference of operator task-level intent and supports assistance over extended tasks.

<br class="ltx_break">(2) Second, we present a <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multi-step shared control policy</span> composed of the grasping stage and the subsequent object interaction stage, providing a unified framework for diverse manipulation tasks. In the grasping stage, the system assists the user by selecting an optimal grasp pose using an off-the-shelf grasp planner. In the object interaction stage, it infers spatial affordances between the grasped object and its target via the interaction graph, and formulates an optimization problem to solve for a suitable pose for interaction. This two-stage design reflects common tool-use patterns and enables generalization to diverse manipulation tasks without predefined task descriptions or demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/1_framework_vertical.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:452/550;" width="452" height="550" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Overview of our TASC framework. The teleoperation loop begins with RGB-D observations of the scene, which are processed jointly with a VLM to construct an interaction graph (see Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Open-vocabulary Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>) encoding object centers, main axes, and functional relationships. In the interaction graph, the <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span> node denotes the grasped object (if any), <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">blue</span> nodes denote task-relevant objects that serve either as grasp targets or as functionally related objects, and <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">gray</span> nodes denote task-irrelevant objects. The inference module (see Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Goal Inference over Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>) fuses this graph with motion cues to estimate a goal distribution. Guided by this distribution, the shared controller (see Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="III-D Shared Control Policy ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-D</span></a>) blends the user’s translational input (Pos) with assistive rotations (Rot): during grasping, it assists with a grasp planner, while during object interaction it enforces alignment of the two objects’ main axes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments, with both simulated and real-world teleoperated manipulation, show that TASC allows users to complete everyday tasks with reduced input burden and improved efficiency, and generalizes across multiple manipulation tasks and object configurations without task-specific training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this paper are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A task-aware shared control framework that infers user intent from historical control behavior and scene semantics, represented via an open-vocabulary interaction graph. This enables inference of multi-step goals without predefined task descriptions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A shared control policy that provides rotation assistance throughout the task: selecting optimal grasp poses during the grasping stage, and solving optimization problems to align objects according to inferred spatial affordances during the object interaction stage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experimental validations on multi-step teleoperation tasks, demonstrating improved task efficiency and reduced user input compared to pure teleoperation and shared control baselines.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RELATED WORK</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shared Control</span> for high-DoF robotic arms has been widely explored to reduce user burden during teleoperation. Early works primarily focus on assisting EEF translation. For example, Gottardi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> propose an artificial potential field augmented with dynamically generated escape points to guide the robot toward likely goals while avoiding obstacles. Beyond planning-based methods, learning-based approaches leverage collected user data to provide predictive assistance. Robot Trajectron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> predicts the future EEF trajectory from recent motion dynamics, enabling proactive support for reaching tasks. Yoneda et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> learn assistive behaviors from demonstrations using diffusion models. However, controlling rotational DoF remains the most unintuitive and effortful aspect of teleoperation. Existing studies address this by providing predefined EEF goal poses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> or discrete action templates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. While effective in structured settings, these approaches struggle to generalize to everyday manipulation tasks. Another line of work mitigates this challenge by introducing mode-switching strategies between translation and rotation control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, allowing efficient assistance under motion-based user input, but still placing the burden of precise orientation control on the operator.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to limited support for rotation, most existing methods also restrict the task scope to short-horizon behaviors such as reaching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, grasping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, or obstacle avoidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. This narrow focus limits their applicability in real-world scenarios. Providing task-level assistance requires richer contextual information beyond user input history. Recent work such as VOSA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> takes a step in this direction by leveraging vision-based object detection to infer user intent in a zero-shot manner. However, it still offers limited support for post-grasp object interactions and underutilizes semantic cues available from modern foundation models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph-based Representations</span> are well-suited for modeling object relationships. In indoor navigation, scene graphs have been used to represent spatial layouts and semantic relationships to support language-guided path planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In manipulation, action-conditioned scene graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> have enabled interactive exploration and planning over long-horizon tasks by capturing how object states and relationships evolve in response to robot actions. Compared to prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> that primarily model spatial relations (e.g., on, inside), we focus on capturing potential functional interactions between object pairs. In this direction, prior efforts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> in object-object interactions (OOI) have proposed taxonomies and datasets to classify spatio-temporal relationships between objects. However, their applications in robotic manipulation remain underexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Affordance for Manipulation</span> provides actionable priors that support tool usage and motion planning. Early works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> focus on learning single-object affordances through data collection and supervised training, while recent efforts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> extend to modeling object-object affordances. However, these approaches are constrained by data collection efficiency and limited generalization. The emergence of foundation models has made zero-shot affordance inference increasingly feasible because of their ability for semantic scene understanding and commonsense reasoning. Recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> use VLMs to extract keypoint constraints between objects under task instructions. However, due to the limited spatial grounding capabilities of current VLMs, the resulting keypoints can be unstable or inconsistent across queries. Inspired by OmniManip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we instead extract task-relevant pose alignment constraints between object pairs via VLMs. This provides a stable and concise representation that is well-suited for assisting rotation in shared control.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">METHOD</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section first formalizes the problem of multi-step teleoperation under shared control. To provide general-purpose, long-horizon assistance, we introduce TASC (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), a framework consisting of: (1) an open-vocabulary interaction graph that encodes functional object relationships (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Open-vocabulary Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>); (2) a goal inference module that maintains a belief over the user’s task-level intent (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Goal Inference over Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>); and (3) a shared control policy that provides rotation assistance during both grasping and post-grasp object interaction (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="III-D Shared Control Policy ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Formulation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the problem of shared control teleoperation for multi-step manipulation tasks. The user operates the robot via a hand-operated input device. We assume a finite task space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> objects, where each task involves functionally meaningful interaction between objects in the scene (e.g., placing a banana on a plate). The user intends to complete a task in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and this intent is unknown to the system. To reduce user burden, our aim is to allow the user to control only the translational DoF of the EEF, with the system providing rotation assistance as needed. We seek to achieve this in a zero-shot manner, representing an essential step toward practical, everyday use of shared control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During teleoperation, the user issues a sequence of control inputs <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{h}=[x,y,z]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{h}=[x,y,z]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the desired translational motion of the EEF. Our objective is to infer the user’s intended task within the unknown task space <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by leveraging both <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and affordance relationships between objects. The system then generates assistive rotational commands <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{r}=[\alpha,\beta,\gamma]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{r}=[\alpha,\beta,\gamma]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to support effective execution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Open-vocabulary Interaction Graph</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To support task-level intent inference, we construct an <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">open-vocabulary interaction graph</span> that encodes possible object-object functional relationships, which is shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Open-vocabulary Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We define an interaction as a functional composition between two objects that supports a meaningful task, represented as a triplet: <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">object_A – action verb – object_B</em> (e.g., “hammer-hit-nail”).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/2_scene_graph_real_world_two_stage.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:452/649;" width="452" height="649" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>The interaction graph with nodes in different activation states: (a) During grasping, nodes with outgoing edges (i.e., functional tools) are marked active; (b) during object interaction, only the neighbors of the grasped node are considered.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given an RGB image <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> capturing the workspace objects <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}=\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we query a VLM to extract a set of object names and candidate interaction triplets. Unlike typical object recognition pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> that attempt to detect all objects and categorize them, we prompt the model to identify only objects that are functionally relevant, which avoids the need to tune recognition granularity manually. We then construct a directed graph <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, where each node <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in V</annotation></semantics></math> stores the spatial and geometric information of an object <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}\in\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}\in\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each edge <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{ij}\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{ij}\in E</annotation></semantics></math> represents an interaction from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> via an action verb.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To ground the graph in the physical world, we apply GroundedSAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> using the object names to segment masks <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each mask <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute its center and project it into the world coordinate frame <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> via a calibrated RGB-D camera to obtain a 3D position <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also extract a partial point cloud <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{pcd}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>pcd</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{pcd}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for each object. The information <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{m_{j},p_{j},\text{pcd}_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>pcd</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{m_{j},p_{j},\text{pcd}_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is stored in the corresponding node <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This interaction graph serves as a compact representation of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, providing high-level semantic context for downstream shared control modules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Goal Inference over Interaction Graph</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generalize across diverse tasks, we decompose multi-step manipulation tasks into two stages—<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">grasping</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">object interaction</span>—and infer the operation goal in each stage. Prior methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> typically treat all visible objects as candidate goals, which can be inefficient and ambiguous in cluttered scenes. In contrast, we narrow the candidate goal set using the interaction graph. Our interaction graph encodes pairwise object relationships, which align naturally with the two stages of the teleoperated manipulation task. During grasping, only nodes with non-zero out-degree are considered, representing objects that can act on others. During object interaction, only nodes directly connected to the grasped object are included in the candidate goal set <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To infer the goal <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G</annotation></semantics></math> in the current stage, we estimate the probability <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})</annotation></semantics></math> at timestep <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, given the sequence of robot states and user inputs <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi^{0:t}=\{x^{0},u_{h}^{0},\dots,x^{t},u_{h}^{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{0:t}=\{x^{0},u_{h}^{0},\dots,x^{t},u_{h}^{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The robot state <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> contains EEF position and orientation. By adopting the Bayes rule, we can obtain:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})\propto p(\xi^{0:t}|g)p(g)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})\propto p(\xi^{0:t}|g)p(g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming that the user input is only dependent on the current goal <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and robot state <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can define <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\xi^{0:t}|g)=\prod_{t}p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\xi^{0:t}|g)=\prod_{t}p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using uniform prior for probability <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g)</annotation></semantics></math> for each goal initially, we can rewrite (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III-C Goal Inference over Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})\propto p(\xi^{0:t-1}|g)p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})\propto p(\xi^{0:t-1}|g)p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This recursive form allows <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})</annotation></semantics></math> to be updated online at each time step based on the current observation <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use MaxEnt IOC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> to model the likelihood <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-C Goal Inference over Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which assumes the user acts to minimize a goal-specific cost <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to a distribution where lower-cost actions are exponentially more likely:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)\propto\exp\left(V_{g}(x^{t})-(C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})+V_{g}((x^{t})^{\prime}))\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(u_{h}^{t}|x^{t},g)\propto\exp\left(V_{g}(x^{t})-(C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})+V_{g}((x^{t})^{\prime}))\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g}(x^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g}(x^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the expected remaining cost-to-go to reach <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{t})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{t})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the robot state after applying <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{h}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{h}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We adopt a similar formulation of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Specifically, we define <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> based on the translational distance <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from the current EEF pose to goal <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}(x^{t},u^{t}_{h})=\begin{cases}\frac{c_{0}}{\delta}d_{g}(x^{t}),&amp;d_{g}(x^{t})\leq\delta\\
c_{0},&amp;d_{g}(x^{t})&gt;\delta\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}(x^{t},u^{t}_{h})=\begin{cases}\frac{c_{0}}{\delta}d_{g}(x^{t}),&amp;d_{g}(x^{t})\leq\delta\\
c_{0},&amp;d_{g}(x^{t})&gt;\delta\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant offset and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a distance threshold. This user cost is constant when far from the goal (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g}(x^{t})&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g}(x^{t})&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>), encouraging fast and steady progress, and linear when near the goal (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g}(x^{t})\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g}(x^{t})\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>), reflecting slower, fine-grained adjustment. With this definition, we compute <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g}(x^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g}(x^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}(x^{t},u_{h}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by scaling it with a distance-dependent factor <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(k\ d_{g}(x^{t})+b\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(k\ d_{g}(x^{t})+b\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are constants chosen to yield a quadratic profile near the goal and a linear profile far from the goal, capturing rapid cost changes during fine alignment and steady changes during approach, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the posterior probability <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained at every step by accumulating (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-C Goal Inference over Interaction Graph ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) over time. Compared to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we assist only in orientation to avoid the feedback bias introduced by translation assistance. The resulting distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})</annotation></semantics></math> guides downstream shared control policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Shared Control Policy</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grasping Assistance</span> is designed to provide lightweight yet precise rotation support during the grasping stage. We leverage a pretrained grasp planner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> to generate candidate grasp poses for each task-relevant object. To ensure both diversity and spatial coverage, we apply farthest point sampling to dense predictions, retaining <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly distributed grasp poses per object. At runtime, the system maintains a belief distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})</annotation></semantics></math> over candidate objects. The most probable object <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{*}=\arg\max p(g|\xi^{0:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mi>max</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}=\arg\max p(g|\xi^{0:t})</annotation></semantics></math> is selected, and we compute the Euclidean distance between each of its candidate grasp poses and the current EEF pose. For the closest grasp, we also consider its equivalent pose obtained by rotating <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="180^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">180^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> about the gripper approach axis, and select whichever requires less rotation as the assistive target <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The system interpolates between the EEF pose and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and modulates rotational assistance proportionally to the user’s translational control magnitude. Fine adjustments near the grasp point are often the most challenging and critical for grasp success. Once the user triggers the gripper-close action, the system enters a fully autonomous mode, taking over both translation and rotation to smoothly complete the final approach and execute the grasp.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/3_spatial_affordance.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/350;" width="452" height="350" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Object interaction assistance via affordance-based axis alignment. The system uses a VLM to infer axis alignment constraints between the grasped object and the target, and solves for the optimal rotation to satisfy these constraints.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object Interaction Assistance</span> is activated after a successful grasp. Manipulation tasks involving functional object interactions usually require specific pose relationships between objects. These requirements can be modeled as desired alignments between their local coordinate frames. To enable such assistance, we identify the interaction pair from <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>: the grasped object <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and its associated target object <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-D Shared Control Policy ‣ III METHOD ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. For each object, we compute its canonical axes (X, Y, Z) using the oriented bounding box from its point cloud. We then render axis-overlaid object images and prompt a VLM to identify which axes should be aligned or anti-aligned. This produces a set of constraints <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{c_{i}=(a_{i},b_{i},s_{i})\mid i=1,\dots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{c_{i}=(a_{i},b_{i},s_{i})\mid i=1,\dots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies that axis <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> should be aligned (if <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) or anti-aligned (if <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=-1</annotation></semantics></math>) with axis <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of predicted constraints. Given the estimated poses of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we formulate an optimization problem to compute the minimal rotation <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}\in SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}\in SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> that best satisfies the desired axis alignments:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{R_{g}\in SO(3)}\sum_{c_{i}\in\mathcal{C}}\left\|R_{g}\cdot a_{i}-s_{i}\cdot b_{i}\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></munder><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{R_{g}\in SO(3)}\sum_{c_{i}\in\mathcal{C}}\left\|R_{g}\cdot a_{i}-s_{i}\cdot b_{i}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">During shared control, this predicted rotation is blended with the user’s translational input to support precise alignment. This module extends shared control beyond grasping, enabling task-aware assistance for post-grasp object interaction. We validate its effectiveness in multi-step manipulation tasks such as placing and hammering, both in simulation and on real hardware.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EXPERIMENTS</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the performance of TASC, we conducted both simulation studies and real-world shared control experiments. All experiments were performed on the Franka Research 3 robotic platform. For our method, we used GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> to extract task-relevant object relations for constructing the interaction graph. Prompts used for querying the model are provided in our code repository.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experiment in the Simulation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method in a tabletop teleoperation environment to answer three main questions: (1) Can TASC achieve accurate task-level intent inference? (2) Can TASC improve efficiency and reduce input effort? (3) Can TASC enhance user experience in teleoperated manipulation?</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation Setup.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-A Experiment in the Simulation ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we built a tabletop manipulation environment using Robosuite v1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> with six objects: banana, plate, marker, mug, hammer, and a wooden block with a peg. All object models are from the YCB dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, except that the wooden block is customized by attaching a cylindrical mesh to simulate the peg. We defined three multi-step manipulation tasks of increasing difficulty:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Place</span>: Place the banana onto the plate. Considered successful if the banana’s center lies within the plate region. This is a simple pick-and-place task with minimal orientation requirements.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insert</span>: Insert the marker into the mug. Requires the pen to be grasped and placed nearly upright inside the mug, demanding accurate orientation alignment.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hammer</span>: Hit the peg with the hammer. Success requires the hammerhead to contact the peg head with an orientation error below 10°, simulating a precise downward strike.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">All tasks involve both grasping and post-grasp object interaction. The user and the system both observe the environment from a single calibrated top-down RGB-D view. Notably, our system has no access to any prior knowledge about object properties or task details.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/4_exp1_setting.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/281;" width="452" height="281" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Simulation setup and initial states of the objects for multi-step shared control tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Protocol.</span> The environment provides a teleoperation interface enabling users to control the EEF position, orientation, and the gripper state via keyboard. Each trial starts with a fixed initial EEF pose, while object poses are randomized. We recruited 10 novice participants from the local community. Each received brief training to operate the robot using a keyboard. During the formal experiment, participants performed the three tasks in a randomized order, and the order of shared control methods was also randomized.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines and Metrics.</span>
We compared TASC with three baselines.</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Teleop.</span> Pure user control without any assistance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TASC<sup id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-</span></sup>.</span> An ablation of our method without using the interaction graph. In the grasping stage, any object with at least one valid grasp predicted by the grasp planner is treated as a candidate goal. In the object interaction stage, all remaining objects on the table (except the grasped one) are included in the candidate goal set, regardless of functionality.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VOSA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</span> A vision-only, zero-shot shared autonomy framework that supports user intent recognition and pick-and-place assistance. It assists only with position control and assumes a predefined set of possible placement locations. To minimize comparison bias, we implement the same perception module (GroundedSAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>) for VOSA as in TASC.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For each trial, we recorded the completion time, trajectory length of the EEF, and the number of control inputs (button presses) during the task execution. In addition to these objective indicators, we also collected four subjective indicators through a questionnaire, each scored on a scale from 0 to 10:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Control: I felt in control of the robot.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Expectation: The robot did what I wanted.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Usability: If I was going to teleoperate a robotic arm, I would like to use this system.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Satisfaction: I was satisfied with my performance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Performance across teleoperation tasks. Each cell shows (Time (s) / Trajectory Length (m) / Inputs).</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Method</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Banana–Plate</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Marker–Mug</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Hammer–Peg</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Teleop</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">54.7</span> / <span id="S4.T1.2.2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.50</span> / 135.4</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">99.9 / 2.16 / 228.3</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">112.9 / 3.40 / 338.6</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">VOSA</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">61.9 / 1.57 / 136.9</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">97.8 / 2.41 / 222.0</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">132.9 / 3.80 / 341.2</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">TASC<sup id="S4.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T1.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-</span></sup></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">91.0 / 2.26 / 178.7</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">124.5 / 2.99 / 267.9</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">73.9 / <span id="S4.T1.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.42</span> / 218.0</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">TASC</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">66.4 / 1.71 / <span id="S4.T1.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">116.6</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">54.7</span> / <span id="S4.T1.2.5.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.52</span> / <span id="S4.T1.2.5.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95.6</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">72.1</span> / <span id="S4.T1.2.5.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.42</span> / <span id="S4.T1.2.5.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">186.7</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and Analysis.</span>
Results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-A Experiment in the Simulation ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>. TASC consistently achieves fewer control inputs across all tasks, indicating lower user burden. In the simple <em id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">banana–plate</em> task which primarily involves straightforward reaching, the advantage of our method is less evident; however, TASC still reduces user inputs compared to pure teleoperation. For the more challenging <em id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">marker–mug</em> and <em id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hammer–peg</em> tasks involving precise alignment, TASC achieves faster task execution efficiency and leads to shorter trajectories. Compared to VOSA, which lacks reasoning about object-object interactions, TASC leverages spatial affordances to alleviate the most fatiguing rotational controls and accelerate task progression. In contrast to its ablated version, TASC benefits from semantic reasoning to guide task-level intent inference, enabling faster and intuitive teleoperation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/5_exp1_prob.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/347;" width="452" height="347" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Goal probability distribution over time across three shared control methods; shaded regions denote correct goal predictions during the grasping and object interaction phases.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We also visualize the evolution of the predicted goal distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(g|\xi^{0:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(g|\xi^{0:t})</annotation></semantics></math> in Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-A Experiment in the Simulation ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, using a representative hammer-hit-peg task. Each colored area represents the probability assigned to a candidate goal, while shaded regions indicate periods in which the system correctly identifies the intended object during the grasping (hammer) and object interaction (peg) stages. Our method exhibits early convergence to the correct goal and maintains stable predictions throughout. In contrast, baseline methods (TASC<sup id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-</span></sup> and VOSA) show delayed convergence and fluctuating predictions, particularly during the object interaction stage. This indicates that our intent inference mechanism not only improves accuracy, but also enhances the temporal consistency of assistance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/Figures/6_objective_metrics.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/205;" width="476" height="205" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>User study results on control, expectation, usability, and satisfaction.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-A Experiment in the Simulation ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, TASC achieves consistently higher ratings on most subjective metrics compared to the baselines. While pure teleoperation naturally offers the highest sense of control, TASC maintains a comparable level in this metric and improves how well the robot behaves as expected, demonstrating a favorable balance between user control and assistance. Furthermore, TASC outperforms all baselines in both usability and satisfaction, confirming its effectiveness in enhancing the overall teleoperation experience.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experiment in the Real-World</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this real-world experiment, we focus on evaluating the generalization capability of TASC, i.e., whether it can handle unseen real-world scenes without any task-specific tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Settings.</span> The experiments were conducted in a tabletop workspace as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-B Experiment in the Real-World ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, with a top-down view provided by an Intel RealSense L515 camera mounted in front of the robot. We prepared diverse everyday manipulation tasks with varying object interactions and configurations, while both objects and task details were unknown to TASC. The user controlled the robot EEF translation, rotation, and gripper state using a joystick, with the left side of the joystick assigned to rotation and the right side to translation. A TASC-assisted task is considered successful if the user can complete the task using only the right side of the joystick.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/7_real_world_setting.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/326;" width="452" height="326" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Real-world experimental setup with a Franka Research 3 arm, tabletop objects, and a top-down RGB-D camera.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/8_real_world_snapshot_short.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/355;" width="452" height="355" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>Snapshots of successful shared-control task executions with TASC, where semi-transparent objects indicate initial positions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IV-B Experiment in the Real-World ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, TASC demonstrates strong generalization across a wide range of real-world tasks, including placing, aligning, and stacking. Despite significant variations in object geometry and interaction patterns, TASC successfully assists users in completing these tasks without any task-specific tuning. Compared to pure teleoperation, users can complete tasks with one hand by controlling only simple translational motions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluate TASC under variations in initial object placements and multi-object interaction scenarios. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ IV-B Experiment in the Real-World ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>(a), three representative tasks are demonstrated: sweeping with a brush, inserting a roller into a holder, and pouring with a kettle. TASC generalizes to different object configurations without task-specific training. In Fig. <a href="#S4.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ IV-B Experiment in the Real-World ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ TASC: Task-Aware Shared Control for Teleoperated Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>(b), both the remote controller and the kettle interact sequentially with multiple objects. TASC correctly predicts the intended interaction pairs and adapts its assistance accordingly, thereby enabling users to accomplish long-horizon manipulation tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10416v1/9_real_world_snapshot.png" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:452/753;" width="452" height="753" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 9: </span>Real-world shared-control experiments with TASC, where semi-transparent objects indicate initial positions: (a) variations in object configurations; (b) multi-object, multi-step manipulations.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CONCLUSIONS</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented a task-aware shared control framework for everyday multi-step manipulation. Our key insight is to leverage foundation models to extract task-relevant object relations and spatial affordances, which are structured into an open-vocabulary interaction graph. This enables efficient goal inference and assistance during both grasping and object interaction. We demonstrated that our system improves task execution efficiency and reduces user input effort compared to existing shared control baselines. One current limitation is that our stage decomposition may not generalize well to tasks involving articulated objects, such as opening drawers. In future work, we plan to extend our framework to more complex tool-use scenarios, explore integration with other input modalities such as language, and study the applicability of our framework to BCI-controlled manipulation.</p>
</div>
</section>
